fact benchmarks {
  title    = "Benchmarks vs Lua / Rhai / Rune"
  related  = [milestones, not_in_v1]
  audience = :both
  body {
    p "Microbenchmarks against the languages wscript competes with for the embedding niche. Honest numbers, whatever they are. Reproduce with `cargo run --release -p wscript-benchmarks`; each engine runs an equivalent program, only execution is timed (compilation excluded), best of 5, release profile. Sources live in `benchmarks/src/main.rs`."
    p "Measured 2026-06-12 on Linux 7.0.10-2-cachyos, 13th Gen Intel Core i7-13700H, rune 0.14.2, rustc 1.92.0."
    table {
      header = ["benchmark", "wscript", "Lua 5.4 (mlua)", "Rhai", "Rune"]
      rows = [
        ["fib(25) recursive",        "30.78ms", "3.86ms", "78.41ms",  "13.06ms"],
        ["loop sum 1..=2,000,000",   "94.49ms", "7.09ms", "316.11ms", "92.02ms"],
        ["string build (20k pieces)", "4.26ms", "4.30ms", "62.26ms",  "10.15ms"],
        ["list sort (10k ints)",      "1.37ms", "2.49ms", "4.62ms",   "1.62ms"],
      ]
    }
    h2 "Reading the numbers"
    p "**Lua wins raw interpretation** (function calls, hot loops) by roughly 3–13× — thirty years of C-interpreter tuning versus a plain-enum register VM with no NaN-boxing and no dispatch tricks (deliberate: start simple, benchmark later). **wscript beats Rhai on every benchmark** (3–15×). **Rune 0.14 and wscript trade blows**: Rune's tuned call path wins recursive fib (~2.4×), the loop is a wash, and wscript wins the builtin-dominated workloads (string build ~2.4×, sort ~1.2×) where its statically-selected typed instructions and native routines pay off."
    h2 "Known headroom (v2 candidates)"
    p "NaN-boxed values, inline caching of call frames, fused compare-and-branch instructions, and avoiding per-instruction frame re-borrowing in the dispatch loop."
  }
}
